Never raise from the branch that exists to report a bad argument.

-[NSFileManager attributesOfItemAtPath:error:] checks for a nil path and then builds its NSError
userInfo as @{ NSFilePathErrorKey: path } with that same nil as the value. A dictionary literal
with a nil value RAISES, so the guard written to answer a nil path politely was what killed the
caller:

    Terminating app due to uncaught exception NSException, reason:
    Tried to init dictionary with nil object at index 0, for key NSFilePath

iTerm2 walks into it from -[iTermFileDescriptorMultiClient shouldCopyServerTo:] when it cannot work
out where its server binary lives, and it dies there rather than taking the nil return it was
already written to handle. Measured: 3 runs of 3 terminated before, 0 of 3 after.

There is no path to report when the path is nil, so the key is left out and the code says
NSFileReadInvalidFileNameError, which is what the caller actually wants to know.

THIS IS NOT WHAT LEFT ITERM2 WITHOUT A SHELL, and the distinction cost an A/B to establish. Its
server binary is MISSING from the staged bundle: Contents/MacOS holds iTerm2 and nothing else,
because systemd sweeps /tmp by age and the prefixes live there, so the file went while the main
binary stayed young by being read every run. That is the case scripts/app-stage.sh was written for.
No iTerm2 source archive is left on this machine to re-stage from.

--- a/src/NSFileManager.m
+++ b/src/NSFileManager.m
@@ -635,9 +635,18 @@
 {
     if (path == nil) {
         if (error) {
+            /* THE GUARD WAS THE CRASH. This branch exists because the path is nil, and it then
+             * built a dictionary literal with that nil as the value for NSFilePathErrorKey, which
+             * raises "Tried to init dictionary with nil object at index 0, for key NSFilePath".
+             * So every caller that asked about a nil path was killed by the code meant to answer
+             * it politely. iTerm2 does exactly that from shouldCopyServerTo: when it cannot work
+             * out where its server binary is, and died on the spot instead of taking the error
+             * return it was already written to handle.
+             *
+             * There is no path to report, so the key is simply left out. */
             *error = [NSError errorWithDomain: NSCocoaErrorDomain
-                                         code: NSFileReadUnknownError
-                                     userInfo: @{ NSFilePathErrorKey: path }];
+                                         code: NSFileReadInvalidFileNameError
+                                     userInfo: nil];
         }
         return nil;
     }
